1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:04,917
Imagine you wake up tomorrow, reach for
your phone, and nothing loads.

2
00:00:05,187 --> 00:00:09,983
Not your Wi-Fi. Not your phone company.
The entire internet is gone.

3
00:00:10,253 --> 00:00:13,883
And it's not coming back for one whole
year.

4
00:00:14,153 --> 00:00:20,117
Five and a half billion people just lost
the first thing they check every morning.

5
00:00:20,387 --> 00:00:26,717
So what would actually happen? On day one,
in week one, and over the whole year?

6
00:00:26,987 --> 00:00:32,950
First, the rules. Electricity still works.
So do radio, TV broadcasts, and old
landline phones.

7
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:39,183
But anything that sends data over the
internet, from apps to bank transfers,
simply stops.

8
00:00:39,453 --> 00:00:43,483
Let's start at seven a.m. on the very
first morning.

9
00:00:43,753 --> 00:00:48,150
Pip's first move is the same as yours.
Restart the router.

10
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:52,050
Twice. Then a third time, a little more
angrily.

11
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:56,717
Across the street, every neighbor is doing
exactly the same thing.

12
00:00:56,987 --> 00:01:01,783
Then Pip switches on an old radio, and
hears news nobody expected.

13
00:01:02,053 --> 00:01:06,450
It's global. Every country, every network,
all at the same moment.

14
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:13,450
That's stranger than it sounds. Almost all
data between continents travels through
cables on the ocean floor.

15
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:18,117
They carry video calls, card payments,
stock trades, even this video.

16
00:01:18,387 --> 00:01:20,083
Today, they carry nothing.

17
00:01:20,353 --> 00:01:23,617
By eight a.m., the morning commute falls
apart.

18
00:01:23,887 --> 00:01:27,517
Ride apps are dead. Ticket gates won't
read phones.

19
00:01:27,787 --> 00:01:34,117
Millions of people realize they don't
actually know the way to work without a
map app.

20
00:01:34,387 --> 00:01:41,117
GPS satellites still work, by the way. But
without data, most maps can't even load
the streets.

21
00:01:41,387 --> 00:01:46,183
So people do something they haven't done
in years. They ask strangers.

22
00:01:46,453 --> 00:01:50,083
Then comes the first real shock. Paying
for things.

23
00:01:50,353 --> 00:01:55,550
Card machines need a network to check your
bank. No network, no payment.

24
00:01:55,820 --> 00:02:01,783
Some shops can accept cards offline, but
only small amounts, and only for a while.

25
00:02:02,053 --> 00:02:06,850
Everyone else needs cash. And everyone
needs it at the same time.

26
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:13,083
Many cash machines run on networks too, so
the lines move to the bank branches.

27
00:02:13,353 --> 00:02:18,550
By lunchtime, a handful of cash is the
most valuable thing in town.

28
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:23,217
Pip works from home, on video calls. Or
rather, used to.

29
00:02:23,487 --> 00:02:30,617
Offices, schools and online stores go
quiet. Online shopping alone is worth
around six trillion dollars a year.

30
00:02:30,887 --> 00:02:34,150
Every one of those orders just froze, mid-
click.

31
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:40,383
Airports have it even worse. Flights
depend on networks for booking, check-in
and crew schedules.

32
00:02:40,653 --> 00:02:49,717
We've had a preview. In 2024, one faulty
software update crashed about eight and a
half million computers and grounded
thousands of flights.

33
00:02:49,987 --> 00:02:54,383
That chaos lasted a few days. This one
lasts a year.

34
00:02:54,653 --> 00:02:58,283
Hospitals still have power, and doctors
still know medicine.

35
00:02:58,553 --> 00:03:02,950
But patient records, lab results and
prescriptions live in connected systems.

36
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:08,417
So they switch to backups. Printed charts,
handwritten orders, and runners carrying
folders.

37
00:03:08,687 --> 00:03:11,950
By the first night, the city feels
strange.

38
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:17,017
The lights are on. But the windows don't
glow blue with screens.

39
00:03:17,287 --> 00:03:23,617
Pip lies in bed, picks up the phone out of
habit, and puts it back down.

40
00:03:23,887 --> 00:03:26,750
Week one is when things get serious.

41
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:29,117
Supermarket shelves start running thin.

42
00:03:29,387 --> 00:03:34,950
Not because the food ran out, but because
ordering it ran on the internet.

43
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:40,783
Stores normally reorder stock
automatically, often every day. Trucks
follow routes planned by software.

44
00:03:41,053 --> 00:03:46,617
Now managers phone suppliers on landlines,
and fax machines wake up from long naps.

45
00:03:46,887 --> 00:03:49,350
Yes. The fax machine is back.

46
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:55,583
But the bigger problem is information.
Without the internet, how do you know
what's true?

47
00:03:55,853 --> 00:04:01,050
Rumors fly. It'll be back tomorrow. The
banks are closing. Aliens did it.

48
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:06,517
So people turn to the oldest networks we
have. Radio, TV and newspapers.

49
00:04:06,787 --> 00:04:11,983
Printed newspapers, which had been
shrinking for years, sell out by nine a.m.

50
00:04:12,253 --> 00:04:16,650
Town halls pin notices to boards, just
like a century ago.

51
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:20,183
But what about talking to people far away?

52
00:04:20,453 --> 00:04:27,583
Most mobile calls now travel as data.
Whether they still work depends on how
each carrier routes them.

53
00:04:27,853 --> 00:04:33,050
Old landlines hold up better, and lines
form at the last few payphones.

54
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:35,783
And then, something surprising happens.
Letters.

55
00:04:36,053 --> 00:04:43,950
The U.S. Postal Service already moves over
a hundred billion pieces of mail a year.
Now it becomes the internet.

56
00:04:44,220 --> 00:04:51,350
Pip writes a letter for the first time
since school. It takes forty minutes.
Mostly crossing things out.

57
00:04:51,620 --> 00:04:56,017
By the end of the first month, panic turns
into routine.

58
00:04:56,287 --> 00:05:01,850
Shops run on cash and paper receipts.
Banks process transfers on paper again,
slowly.

59
00:05:02,120 --> 00:05:07,317
Companies send couriers on motorbikes
between offices, carrying hard drives full
of data.

60
00:05:07,587 --> 00:05:16,250
Engineers have a name for this:
sneakernet. And for huge amounts of data,
a truck full of drives can beat the
internet.

61
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:21,317
Engineers race to build private links
between hospitals, banks and power grids.

62
00:05:21,587 --> 00:05:27,550
But rebuilding something the size of the
internet? That takes far longer than a
year.

63
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:29,517
So who struggles most?

64
00:05:29,787 --> 00:05:34,983
Online businesses, delivery drivers, app
makers and creators lose their income
almost overnight.

65
00:05:35,253 --> 00:05:41,983
People far from family feel it most. A
weekly video call becomes a letter every
two weeks.

66
00:05:42,253 --> 00:05:43,950
And who suddenly thrives?

67
00:05:44,220 --> 00:05:49,017
Libraries. Bookshops. Local markets. Radio
stations. Anyone who can fix old machines.

68
00:05:49,287 --> 00:05:54,850
Amateur radio operators, once a quiet
hobby, become a lifeline for messages
between towns.

69
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:57,217
And paper maps become bestsellers.

70
00:05:57,487 --> 00:06:01,883
Six months in, something unexpected
happens. People get used to it.

71
00:06:02,153 --> 00:06:11,217
Studies of people who quit social media or
block mobile internet for a while often
find they sleep better and feel less
anxious.

72
00:06:11,487 --> 00:06:16,283
Not everyone, and not right away. But the
constant buzzing is gone.

73
00:06:16,553 --> 00:06:22,117
Neighbors talk more, because they have to.
Streets fill with swap markets and clubs.

74
00:06:22,387 --> 00:06:29,117
Pip and Momo start a weekly news sheet for
their block. Two hundred copies, delivered
by hand.

75
00:06:29,387 --> 00:06:34,583
Kids go back to encyclopedias, and argue
about facts nobody can look up.

76
00:06:34,853 --> 00:06:38,117
Still, let's be honest. The cost is
enormous.

77
00:06:38,387 --> 00:06:45,117
The world economy is built for speed.
Without the internet, trade, research and
medicine all slow down.

78
00:06:45,387 --> 00:06:49,417
Scientists can't share data instantly.
Weather forecasts get less precise.

79
00:06:49,687 --> 00:06:53,717
Many jobs disappear, and millions of
people need new ones.

80
00:06:53,987 --> 00:06:59,550
The world doesn't collapse. But it gets
poorer, slower, and a lot more local.

81
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:02,683
Then, after three hundred and sixty-five
days...

82
00:07:02,953 --> 00:07:06,583
A tiny icon appears on Pip's phone. One
bar.

83
00:07:06,853 --> 00:07:08,550
The internet is back.

84
00:07:08,820 --> 00:07:12,450
And a year's worth of notifications arrive
at once.

85
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:17,117
Would we go right back to normal?
Probably, within a week.

86
00:07:17,387 --> 00:07:19,483
But some things might stick.

87
00:07:19,753 --> 00:07:25,717
Companies would build better backups, so
one failure can't stop a hospital or an
airport.

88
00:07:25,987 --> 00:07:33,483
People might keep some cash at home, a
paper map in the car, and a radio in the
drawer.

89
00:07:33,753 --> 00:07:39,317
And Pip? Pip keeps writing letters to
Momo. Even though they live next door.

90
00:07:39,587 --> 00:07:45,150
Because the strangest part of a year
without the internet wasn't what we lost.

91
00:07:45,420 --> 00:07:49,450
It was what we found when we finally
looked up.

92
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:57,217
Now it's your turn. Could you last one
year offline? Or even one week? Tell me in
the comments.

93
00:07:57,487 --> 00:08:04,617
And if you were the only person on Earth
still online... well, that's a story for
next time.
